ga4c(){return"On the Beat or Off?"},
garE(){return"Straight rhythm, or syncopated?"},
gaxi(){return"Is this rhythm on the beat, or syncopated?"},
gaxj(){return"On the beat"},
gaxk(){return"Syncopated"},
ga4l(){return"Even or Triplet?"},
garN(){return"Is the beat split in two, or in three?"},
gbou(){return"How is the beat split?"},
gbot(){return"Even (2)"},
gbov(){return"Triplet (3)"},
ga3H(){return"Which Ornament?"},
garh(){return"Read the trill, mordent or turn"},
gbba(){return"Which ornament is on the note?"},
gbbb(){return"Trill"},
gbb9(){return"Mordent"},
gbbc(){return"Turn"},
gbf9(){return"On the beat, or off?"},
gbf8(){return"Usually the notes land right ON the beats \u2014 count 1-2-3-4 and each note lands on a number. Steady and square."},
gbf7(){return'Syncopation pushes notes OFF the beat, onto the "and" in between. The accent lands where your ear didnt expect it \u2014 thats the kick you feel in pop and jazz.'},
gbfv(){return"Two or three in a beat"},
gbft(){return'Normally a beat splits into TWO even halves: "1-and". Two eighth notes.'},
gbfu(){return'A triplet squeezes THREE equal notes into that same beat: "trip-o-let". It gets a little 3 above it.'},
gbeq(){return"Decorating a note"},
gber(){return"Ornaments are little signs that dress up a note. A trill (tr) shakes quickly between the note and the one just above it."},
gbes(){return"A turn (a sideways S) curls AROUND the note: the note above, the note, the note below, then back. A mordent is just one quick flick up and back."},
ga30(){return"Label the Form"},
gaqF(){return"Hear the sections; name the shape (ABA\u2026)"},
gb0p(){return"What is the form? (same colour = same tune)"},
gb0o(){return"Listen"},
gbdu(){return"The shape of a piece"},
gbdt(){return"Music is built from sections. Here is a little tune \u2014 call it section A. Whenever it comes back, it is A again."},
gbds(){return"A different tune is a new letter \u2014 section B. Tune, different tune, then the first tune again makes the form A-B-A. Lots of songs are shaped this way!"},
ga3s(){return"Which Mode?"},
gar6(){return"Major, minor or Dorian?"},
gb8Z(){return"Listen! Which mode is it?"},
ga00(){return"Major"},
ga01(){return"Minor"},
gb8Y(){return"Dorian"},
gbem(){return"Three colours of scale"},
gbek(){return"A major scale sounds bright and happy. Listen to it climb."},
gbel(){return"A minor scale sounds darker \u2014 its 3rd, 6th and 7th steps sit a little lower."},
gbej(){return"Dorian is like minor, but its 6th step is raised \u2014 so it sounds minor with a brighter twist. That one note is the whole secret!"},
